THE LAW OF INNOCENCE by Michael Connelly

2020. Allen&Unwin. Paperback.

This was a re-read of a Mickey Haller ("Lincoln Lawyer") thriller and
I'd forgotten all of the plot details so it was just as good as a
first read. Harry Bosch is in this story as well to help Haller's
defence team, but this is really a Lincoln Lawyer story and I find I
enjoy the Mickey Haller stories more; probably because the Bosch
stories have such gruesome crimes.

Michael Connelly has a nice habit of dropping real-world topical
events into his thrillers and during the course of The Law of
Innocence, Covid-19 starts to spread.

The Law of Innocence follows on from events in The Fifth Witness which
I'll re-read later this year and consider as a "prequel" to this
book. I'm pleased to say that the re-readability of Connelly books so
far has proven very high; which is unusual for me with crime stories.
I re-read Joseph Kanon's "Los Alamos" last year and found it
unsatisfying as I could remember "whodunnit".

Micky Haller is pulled over by the cops late at night and a search
of his car reveals a dead body in the trunk. The dead body is an
ex-client of Haller's, a conman who Haller had represented and who
hadn't paid his legal fees. There was bad blood between them, but now
there's actual blood and Haller has been framed for his murder.

There wasn't as much Bosch in the book as I would've liked; indeed the
final twists of the book come from information supplied by the FBI not
from the homicide detective. The plot device of the Mafia running
scams on the government to take biofuels subsidies by running the same
trucks in and out of refineries with no processing taking place I
found very satisfying. The conman in the trunk was running his own
biofuel scam and the mobster who set up the frame of Haller,
Opparizio, was dispatched by higher mob bosses for making things
personal. Let's hear Bosch explain Opparizio's actions to Haller.

    "He let personal business get in the way of real business,"
    Bosch said. "He should have taken care of Scales cleanly. Whacked
    him, buried him, put him in an oil barrell and dropped him in the
    channel. Anything but what he did. He used the situation -- whatever
    it was -- to try and settle an old score with you. That was his
    mistake and it made him vulnerable. He had to go and the thing is,
    he knew it. I don't think he was out in Arizona hiding from you
    and a subpoena. He was hiding from a bullet."

    I nodded. The former homicide detective was very close.

The back cover of my book boasts that my first reading took just two
over a single weekend; this reading took at most four. Connelly
keeps the pages turning.

